From 6da01d306213f1fbe72467bc4d890e38b77f44aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 11:59:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] rtrafd: account traffic from conntrack, not the accelerator
 table

Field report: the Traffic Analyzer is not accurate - devices with large fast
downloads are missing from the list, and br0 looks wrong. The owner's hypothesis
was that only one of the two packet paths is being seen. That is exactly right,
and the source says which one.

WHAT WAS WRONG

rtrafd took its per-device and per-network bytes from /proc/fcache/nflist and
used conntrack only as an all-or-nothing FALLBACK, entered when the flow cache
yielded no flows at all. But nflist lists ACCELERATED flows only. A flow that is
not offloaded - or is merely between evictions - simply is not in that file, so
its bytes were invisible whenever ANY other flow was accelerated, which is the
normal state of the box. The fallback never ran, because "no flows at all" is
rare.

The comment that justified the design was wrong on this platform. It claimed "an
offloaded flow's conntrack bytes freeze at offload, so the two sources never
account the same bytes". On Broadcom HND they do not freeze:

  nf_conntrack_acct.c seq_print_acct()  prints
      software counter + blog_ct_get_stats(<live accelerated bytes>)
  blog.c blog_ct_put_stats()            "when flow is evicted push the flow
                                         stats in to ct"

So /proc/net/nf_conntrack already reports software AND accelerated bytes,
monotonically, with no double counting - it is the COMPLETE source, and it was
the one being left on the shelf.

Large fast downloads were hit twice, which is why they were the visible symptom.
They are the flows most likely to be evicted and re-learned (and with the
transferred-bytes QoS presets fixed earlier today, evicted constantly - see
79036fb47f). On top of that, the flow-cache pass could only attribute a client's
NAT'd download by pairing it to the connection's UPLOAD half through the shared
nf_conn pointer, and required that half to be present in the same 5 s sample with
a non-zero delta. For a big download the upload half is a thin ACK stream that is
routinely absent, evicted, or past the FC_MAX truncation - and then the whole
download was dropped rather than mis-attributed.

WHAT IT DOES NOW

poll_conntrack() runs every sample and owns the client and per-network rings.
poll_fcache() keeps only what the accelerator table can say and conntrack cannot:
the router's own traffic (those flows ride a *_local pseudo-dev and carry no LAN
address) and the per-flow first-seen times the Connections page shows as age. The
two cannot double count: conntrack skips any flow whose source is not on a LAN
bridge, and the flow-cache pass now skips every flow that is.

Per-network attribution needed a way to place a bare address, since conntrack
carries no netdev. Added brsub_refresh()/ip_bridge(): the LAN bridges and their
subnets, read from the live interfaces with SIOCGIFCONF/SIOCGIFNETMASK rather
than from nvram, so guest/SDN bridges are picked up whenever they are created
with no schema knowledge. That also fixes a second, quieter gap - conntrack
attribution tested only br0's subnet, so every guest/SDN client was dropped
outright.

Two things fall out of it:
- br0 is now summed from the same complete source as the device rows, so the
  network total can reconcile with the WAN line instead of counting only the
  offloaded part.
- the router's own LAN address stops being credited as a device row - it goes to
  the Router bucket, which is the [P3] "connection-health probe lists the router
  as a device" backlog item.

NOT BUILT. Cross-compiles clean (aarch64 gcc-10.3, -Wall, no new warnings).
Metal is what settles it, and the check is a reconciliation, not a glance: start a
large download on one client, then confirm the client row, the br0 row and the
WAN line agree to within a few percent over the same window, and that the sum of
device rows plus Router tracks br0. Watch for a double count (device + Router)
and for a guest-network client appearing under its own bridge.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/rtrafd/rtrafd.c | 269 ++++++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 164 insertions(+), 105 deletions(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/rtrafd/rtrafd.c b/release/src/router/rtrafd/rtrafd.c
index 48c844308a..80da9ab8d1 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rtrafd/rtrafd.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rtrafd/rtrafd.c
@@ -43,6 +43,8 @@
 #include <netinet/in.h>
 #include <netinet/ip.h>
 #include <netinet/ip_icmp.h>
+#include <sys/ioctl.h>
+#include <net/if.h>
 
 #include <bcmnvram.h>
 #include <shutils.h>
@@ -159,8 +161,63 @@ static uint64_t last_cls_txb[7], last_cls_dp[7];
 static int have_cls_last = 0;
 
 static uint32_t lan_net = 0, lan_mask = 0;
+static uint32_t lan_ip = 0;		/* the router's OWN LAN address (host order) */
 static volatile sig_atomic_t running = 1;
 
+/* reaper 2026-08-11: every LAN bridge and its subnet, so a client IP alone can
+ * be attributed to its network. The flow cache carries RxDev/TxDev and needed no
+ * such map, but conntrack - which is the only COMPLETE byte source on this
+ * platform (see poll_conntrack) - carries addresses only. Enumerated from the
+ * live interfaces rather than nvram so guest/SDN bridges are picked up whenever
+ * they are created, with no schema knowledge. */
+#define NBRSUB 16
+static struct { char br[16]; uint32_t net, mask; } brsub[NBRSUB];
+static int nbrsub = 0;
+
+static void brsub_refresh(void)
+{
+	struct ifconf ifc;
+	struct ifreq buf[32], ifr;
+	int s, i, n;
+
+	if ((s = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0)) < 0) return;
+	memset(&ifc, 0, sizeof(ifc));
+	ifc.ifc_len = sizeof(buf);
+	ifc.ifc_req = buf;
+	if (ioctl(s, SIOCGIFCONF, &ifc) < 0) { close(s); return; }
+
+	nbrsub = 0;
+	n = ifc.ifc_len / (int)sizeof(struct ifreq);
+	for (i = 0; i < n && nbrsub < NBRSUB; i++) {
+		uint32_t a, m;
+
+		if (strncmp(buf[i].ifr_name, "br", 2) != 0) continue;	/* LAN bridges only */
+		if (buf[i].ifr_addr.sa_family != AF_INET) continue;
+		a = ntohl(((struct sockaddr_in *)&buf[i].ifr_addr)->sin_addr.s_addr);
+
+		memset(&ifr, 0, sizeof(ifr));
+		strlcpy(ifr.ifr_name, buf[i].ifr_name, sizeof(ifr.ifr_name));
+		if (ioctl(s, SIOCGIFNETMASK, &ifr) < 0) continue;
+		m = ntohl(((struct sockaddr_in *)&ifr.ifr_addr)->sin_addr.s_addr);
+		if (!m) continue;
+
+		strlcpy(brsub[nbrsub].br, buf[i].ifr_name, sizeof(brsub[0].br));
+		brsub[nbrsub].mask = m;
+		brsub[nbrsub].net = a & m;
+		nbrsub++;
+	}
+	close(s);
+}
+
+/* which LAN bridge owns this address, or NULL if it is not on any of them */
+static const char *ip_bridge(uint32_t ip)
+{
+	int i;
+	for (i = 0; i < nbrsub; i++)
+		if ((ip & brsub[i].mask) == brsub[i].net) return brsub[i].br;
+	return NULL;
+}
+
 /* probe accumulators for the open 5-min bucket */
 static uint64_t prb_rtt_sum = 0;
 static uint32_t prb_n = 0, prb_lost = 0;
@@ -637,23 +694,16 @@ static void poll_fcache(void)
 	FILE *f = fopen("/proc/fcache/nflist", "r");
 	char line[1024];
 	static struct fcf fl[FC_MAX];
-	static char consumed[FC_MAX];
-	int nfl = 0, i, j;
+	int nfl = 0, i;
 
-	if (!f) { poll_conntrack(); return; }	/* accelerator table absent */
+	if (!f) return;		/* accelerator table absent: conntrack already ran */
 
-	ntop = 0;
-	/* Load the ARP table on THIS path too, not only in the conntrack fallback.
-	 * poll_conntrack() is the fallback (accelerator off / no flows); with the
-	 * accelerator on - the normal state - we return below without ever reaching
-	 * it, so arp_n stayed 0 for the daemon's whole life and every arp_mac()
-	 * lookup in write_live() missed. Top-talker and top-device rows could then
-	 * only ever show raw IPs, never a MAC, and so never resolve to a device
-	 * name. Cheap: one small file, once per 5 s fcache sample. */
-	arp_refresh();
-	/* reset the live per-device/per-network rate accumulators for this sample */
-	memset(lv_cli_rx, 0, sizeof(lv_cli_rx)); memset(lv_cli_tx, 0, sizeof(lv_cli_tx));
-	memset(lv_net_rx, 0, sizeof(lv_net_rx)); memset(lv_net_tx, 0, sizeof(lv_net_tx));
+	/* Runs AFTER poll_conntrack(), which owns the client and per-network rings
+	 * and has already refreshed ARP and zeroed the live accumulators. What is
+	 * left here is only what the accelerator table can say and conntrack cannot:
+	 * the router's OWN traffic (its flows ride a *_local pseudo-dev rather than a
+	 * LAN bridge, so there is no LAN address to attribute) and the per-flow first
+	 * seen times the Connections page shows as flow age. */
 	if (!fgets(line, sizeof(line), f) || !fgets(line, sizeof(line), f)) {
 		fclose(f);		/* two header lines */
 		return;
@@ -718,29 +768,20 @@ static void poll_fcache(void)
 
 	connseen_update(fl, nfl);
 
-	/* nflist yielded no flows-with-deltas. Two cases: (a) genuinely idle, or
-	 * (b) the accelerator is OFF - which is exactly what the software QoS
-	 * engines (Traditional type 0, Cake type 9) do. Either way, fall back to
-	 * conntrack, which sees the software path accurately when accel is off (and
-	 * finds little when merely idle). No double counting: when accel is ON,
-	 * nfl>0 and this is skipped; and an offloaded flow's conntrack bytes freeze
-	 * at offload, so the two sources never account the same bytes. */
-	if (nfl == 0) {
-		poll_conntrack();
+	if (nfl == 0)
 		return;
-	}
 
 	/* age the fc_last table: any slot NOT seen this poll keeps its value, which
 	 * is fine (delta stays 0 until it grows or a new flow recycles the slot). */
 
-	/* ---- Pass 1: per-network attribution + a "Router" bucket, taken straight
-	 *      from each flow's own direction. This needs NO upload<->download
-	 *      correlation, so the per-network (br0) numbers now reflect real LAN
-	 *      traffic, and locally-terminated traffic - the router's OWN speed
-	 *      test / DNS / firmware checks / probe, whose flows ingress or egress
-	 *      a "*_local" pseudo-dev rather than a LAN bridge - stops being
-	 *      invisible. br0 + Router + clients then reconcile with the WAN line,
-	 *      which counts every byte regardless of who sourced it. ---- */
+	/* ---- The router's OWN traffic - its speed test / DNS / firmware checks /
+	 *      health probe. Those flows ingress or egress a "*_local" pseudo-dev
+	 *      rather than a LAN bridge, so they carry no LAN address for conntrack
+	 *      to attribute, and this is the one thing the accelerator table can say
+	 *      that conntrack cannot. Client and LAN-bridge traffic is NOT counted
+	 *      here any more (2026-08-11): conntrack owns those rings, because the
+	 *      accelerator table sees only offloaded flows. Keeping both would either
+	 *      double count or, as it did, undercount. ---- */
 	{
 		int rci = -1;		/* "Router" device slot, allocated on first use */
 		for (i = 0; i < nfl; i++) {
@@ -751,18 +792,10 @@ static void poll_fcache(void)
 
 			if (!dev[0]) continue;
 			br = dev_bridge(dev);
-			if (fl[i].lan) {			/* LAN client -> WAN: upload */
-				ns = net_slot(br);
-				if (ns >= 0) {
-					ring_add(r_net[ns], 0, d);
-					lv_net_tx[ns] += d * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
-				}
-			} else if (br[0] == 'b' && br[1] == 'r') {	/* WAN -> a LAN bridge: client download */
-				ns = net_slot(br);
-				if (ns >= 0) {
-					ring_add(r_net[ns], d, 0);
-					lv_net_rx[ns] += d * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
-				}
+			if (fl[i].lan) {			/* LAN client -> WAN: conntrack's job */
+				continue;
+			} else if (br[0] == 'b' && br[1] == 'r') {	/* WAN -> a LAN bridge: conntrack's job */
+				continue;
 			} else {			/* neither end on a LAN bridge: the router's own traffic */
 				int into_router = (strstr(dev, "local") != NULL);
 				ns = net_slot(RT_NETLBL);
@@ -786,54 +819,43 @@ static void poll_fcache(void)
 		}
 	}
 
-	/* ---- Pass 2: per-DEVICE (client) attribution. A client's download is NAT'd
-	 *      (src = internet, dst = WAN IP, no LAN address), so it is matched back
-	 *      to the client by the nf_conn[0] pointer shared by the connection's two
-	 *      flows. Per-network is already done in Pass 1, so this pass only fills
-	 *      the client rings + top-talkers. ---- */
-	memset(consumed, 0, nfl);
-	for (i = 0; i < nfl; i++) {
-		uint64_t nfc, up = 0, dn = 0;
-		int have_lan = 0, ci;
-		uint32_t cli_ip = 0, dst_ip = 0;
-		int dport = 0;
-		char cli_mac[20] = "", ips[16], dsts[16];
-
-		if (consumed[i]) continue;
-		nfc = fl[i].nfc;
-		/* group both directions of this connection (shared nf_conn) */
-		for (j = i; j < nfl; j++) {
-			if (consumed[j]) continue;
-			if (nfc && fl[j].nfc != nfc) continue;
-			if (!nfc && j != i) continue;	/* nfc==0: don't group blindly */
-			consumed[j] = 1;
-			if (fl[j].lan) {
-				up += fl[j].delta;
-				if (!have_lan) {
-					have_lan = 1;
-					cli_ip = fl[j].sip;
-					dst_ip = fl[j].dip;
-					dport = fl[j].dport;
-					strlcpy(cli_mac, fl[j].mac, sizeof(cli_mac));
-				}
-			} else {
-				dn += fl[j].delta;
-			}
-		}
-		if (!have_lan)
-			continue;	/* no LAN side: router-originated or an unmatched download (both handled in Pass 1) */
-
-		ip2str(cli_ip, ips);
-		ci = cli_slot(cli_mac[0] ? cli_mac : ips, ips);
-		ring_add(r_cli[ci], dn, up);	/* rx=download, tx=upload */
-		lv_cli_rx[ci] += dn * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
-		lv_cli_tx[ci] += up * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
-
-		ip2str(dst_ip, dsts);
-		top_note(ips, dsts, dport, 0, up, dn);
-	}
+	/* The per-DEVICE pass that used to live here is GONE (2026-08-11). It paired
+	 * a client's NAT'd download back to the client through the nf_conn[0] pointer
+	 * shared by the connection's two flows, which meant a download was only ever
+	 * attributed if its UPLOAD half was in the same 5 s sample with a non-zero
+	 * delta. For a large fast download the upload half is a thin ACK stream that
+	 * is routinely absent, evicted, or past the FC_MAX truncation - so the very
+	 * transfers the user most wants to see were the ones most likely to be
+	 * dropped, which is the "devices with large fast downloads are not shown"
+	 * report. conntrack needs no such pairing: it carries both directions'
+	 * byte counters on one line. */
 }
 
+/* THE byte-accounting pass.
+ *
+ * Until 2026-08-11 this was only a FALLBACK, used when /proc/fcache/nflist
+ * yielded nothing - i.e. when the accelerator was off. That was wrong, and it is
+ * the "Traffic Analyzer only sees one of two packet paths" report:
+ *
+ *   - /proc/fcache/nflist lists ACCELERATED flows only. A flow that is not
+ *     offloaded (or is between evictions) simply is not there, so its bytes were
+ *     invisible whenever ANY other flow was accelerated - which is the normal
+ *     state. Large fast downloads are the worst case, because they are exactly
+ *     the flows that get evicted and re-learned (and, with the pre-2026-08-11
+ *     connbytes QoS presets, evicted constantly).
+ *   - conntrack, on THIS kernel, is complete. The old comment here claimed "an
+ *     offloaded flow's conntrack bytes freeze at offload"; that is not true on
+ *     Broadcom HND. nf_conntrack_acct.c seq_print_acct() prints
+ *         software counter + blog_ct_get_stats(<live accelerated bytes>)
+ *     and blog_ct_put_stats() folds the accelerated bytes into the software
+ *     counter when the flow is evicted ("when flow is evicted push the flow
+ *     stats in to ct"). So /proc/net/nf_conntrack already carries BOTH paths,
+ *     monotonically, with no double counting.
+ *
+ * So this pass now runs every sample and owns the client + per-network rings.
+ * poll_fcache() keeps only what conntrack cannot give: the router's own traffic
+ * (whose flows ride a *_local pseudo-dev) and the Connections page's flow ages.
+ */
 static void poll_conntrack(void)
 {
 	FILE *f = fopen("/proc/net/nf_conntrack", "r");
@@ -842,6 +864,7 @@ static void poll_conntrack(void)
 	flowgen++;
 	ntop = 0;
 	arp_refresh();		/* load /proc/net/arp ONCE per poll, not per flow */
+	brsub_refresh();	/* bridges can appear when a guest network is created */
 	while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), f)) {
 		char proto[16] = "", src[64] = "", dst[64] = "";
 		char rsrc[64] = "";
@@ -876,8 +899,19 @@ static void poll_conntrack(void)
 			char kbuf[160], mac[20];
 			int ci;
 
+			uint32_t sip;
+			const char *br;
+
 			if (inet_aton(src, &a) == 0) continue;
-			if ((ntohl(a.s_addr) & lan_mask) != lan_net) continue;
+			sip = ntohl(a.s_addr);
+			/* any LAN bridge, not just br0 - guest/SDN clients live on their
+			   own subnets and used to be dropped here entirely */
+			if ((br = ip_bridge(sip)) == NULL) continue;
+			/* the router's own LAN address is not a client. It reaches here via
+			   the health probe and any router-sourced LAN traffic; crediting it
+			   as a device is the "router appears as a device row" backlog item.
+			   Bucket it with the router instead. */
+			if (lan_ip && sip == lan_ip) br = NULL;
 
 			snprintf(kbuf, sizeof(kbuf), "%d/%s/%s/%d/%d", pnum, src, dst, sport, dport);
 			key = fnv64(kbuf);
@@ -914,21 +948,32 @@ static void poll_conntrack(void)
 			fl->key = key; fl->ob = ob; fl->rb = rb; fl->gen = flowgen;
 
 			if (dob == 0 && drb == 0) continue;
+
+			/* per-network (br0 / guest bridges / Router), from the same complete
+			   source as the per-device numbers - so br0 now reconciles with the
+			   WAN line instead of showing accelerated flows only */
+			{
+				int ns = net_slot(br ? br : RT_NETLBL);
+				if (ns >= 0) {
+					ring_add(r_net[ns], drb, dob);
+					lv_net_rx[ns] += drb * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
+					lv_net_tx[ns] += dob * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
+				}
+			}
+			if (!br) {			/* router-sourced: the Router device bucket */
+				ci = cli_slot(RT_DEVKEY, RT_DEVLBL);
+				ring_add(r_cli[ci], drb, dob);
+				lv_cli_rx[ci] += drb * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
+				lv_cli_tx[ci] += dob * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
+				top_note(src, dst, dport, pnum, dob, drb);
+				continue;
+			}
 			if (!arp_mac(src, mac, sizeof(mac)))
 				strlcpy(mac, src, sizeof(mac));
 			ci = cli_slot(mac, src);
 			ring_add(r_cli[ci], drb, dob);	/* rx=download(reply), tx=upload(orig) */
-			/* Feed the LIVE per-device rates as well. poll_fcache() zeroes
-			 * the lv_cli_ and lv_net_ accumulators at the top of each sample
-			 * and repopulates them on its own path; when it hands off here it has
-			 * already zeroed them and nothing here filled them back in, so the
-			 * live per-device rows read a flat zero for as long as a software
-			 * QoS engine (type 0/9, accelerator off) is selected. Same
-			 * bits/sec convention as the fcache path.
-			 * NOTE: lv_net_* (per-network) is still not filled here - this path
-			 * has no LAN-side device to map to a bridge, unlike the flow cache
-			 * which carries RxDev/TxDev. Per-network live rows therefore remain
-			 * zero on the fallback; the history rings are unaffected. */
+			/* live per-device rates, same bits/sec convention as the rings.
+			 * The caller zeroes the lv_* accumulators before this pass. */
 			lv_cli_rx[ci] += drb * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
 			lv_cli_tx[ci] += dob * 8 / FCACHE_SECS;
 			top_note(src, dst, dport, pnum, dob, drb);
@@ -1725,8 +1770,10 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
 		    inet_aton(nvram_safe_get("lan_netmask"), &m)) {
 			lan_mask = ntohl(m.s_addr);
 			lan_net = ntohl(a.s_addr) & lan_mask;
+			lan_ip = ntohl(a.s_addr);	/* the router itself is not a client */
 		}
 	}
+	brsub_refresh();	/* LAN bridge subnets, for conntrack's network attribution */
 
 	/* v2.1.5 (field: "JFFS/USB history not persistent"): the store medium is NOT
 	 * guaranteed mounted when we start. USB in particular mounts via hotplug AFTER
@@ -1845,9 +1892,21 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
 			tcls_prev = tcls; have_tcls = 1;
 		}
 
-		/* flow cache (per-device/per-network/top-talkers) + probe: slow cadence */
-		if (tick % HEAVY_EVERY == 0)
+		/* per-device / per-network / top-talkers + probe: slow cadence.
+		 * ORDER MATTERS (2026-08-11): poll_conntrack() is the complete byte
+		 * source (software path AND accelerated bytes - the Broadcom kernel
+		 * folds the fast-path counters into the conntrack readout) and it owns
+		 * the client and network rings, so it zeroes the live accumulators and
+		 * runs first. poll_fcache() then adds only the router's own traffic and
+		 * refreshes the Connections page's flow ages. */
+		if (tick % HEAVY_EVERY == 0) {
+			memset(lv_cli_rx, 0, sizeof(lv_cli_rx));
+			memset(lv_cli_tx, 0, sizeof(lv_cli_tx));
+			memset(lv_net_rx, 0, sizeof(lv_net_rx));
+			memset(lv_net_tx, 0, sizeof(lv_net_tx));
+			poll_conntrack();
 			poll_fcache();
+		}
 		if (tick % PROBE_EVERY == 0)
 			poll_probe();
 
-- 
